        "marks": 6,
        "model": "Faraday's first law of electrolysis states that the mass of a substance deposited (or discharged) at an electrode during electrolysis is directly proportional to the quantity of electric charge (or amount of current) passed through the electrolyte. Mathematically: mass ∝ charge, or mass = (charge/96500) × (molar mass/electrons transferred). The charge is measured in coulombs; one mole of electrons (one Faraday, 96500 C) produces a fixed amount of chemical change at the electrode, regardless of the electrolyte or conditions used.",
        "diagram": "",
