        "question": "Classify the reactions X, Y and Z as substitution, addition or elimination reactions.",
        "marks": 18,
        "model": "Reaction X (CH₃CH₂Br → CH₂=CH₂): Elimination reaction. Under suitable conditions (heat, alcoholic KOH), the H-C-C-Br system eliminates HBr, forming a C=C double bond.\n\nReaction Y (CH₂=CH₂ → CH₃CH₂OH): Addition reaction. The C=C π-bond opens and water adds across it. H adds to one carbon and OH to the other via an electrophilic addition mechanism, forming a single C-C bond.\n\nReaction Z (CH₃CH₂Br → CH₃CH₂OH): Substitution reaction. The Br nucleophile is displaced by OH⁻ (from aqueous NaOH) via an SN2 mechanism, with OH⁻ attacking the carbon bearing the Br.",
